## What I did

Added an idempotency guard to auto_risk_snapshot() in `src/commands_risk_snapshots.rs`:

1. Pure helper `last_snapshot_git_hash(content)` — reads the last non-blank JSONL line and extracts its git_hash field, returning None for empty/malformed/missing-field content. Small and fully testable without touching the real repo.

2. **Dedup guard in `auto_risk_snapshot()** — I moved the git_hash` computation to the top, then before writing: if the hash is a real one (not "unknown") and matches the last snapshot's hash, it returns early with a dim skip note. "unknown" states are never deduped, since two failed rev-parses may genuinely differ.

3. **6 unit tests** covering the basic case, empty content, trailing-blank-line handling, malformed last line, missing field, and the same-hash-vs-different-hash dedup decision.

4. CLAUDE.md — updated the commands_risk_snapshots.rs bullet to note the dedup behavior.

## Why it matters

A snapshot means "predictions AS OF this HEAD" — so re-recording the same HEAD is pure double-counting. In the evolve loop (multiple commits per session: safety commits + per-task commits), the old unconditional append would have polluted the accumulation the DREAM.md milestone depends on. Now every accumulated point represents a distinct commit-state, so when the accuracy trend finally has enough data to read, it reflects real prediction quality rather than write frequency. That's the allostatic self-model needing clean signal to measure whether the reflex actually works.

